data.content:
Your library is tiny — one call to `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` in `InvoiceCalculator`. The lightest fix on **.NET 8+** is the built-in `TimeProvider` abstract class — no custom interface needed.

**What to do:**

1. Accept `TimeProvider` via constructor (defaults to `TimeProvider.System`):

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;
    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

2. In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.IsTrue(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.IsFalse(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Install `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` in your **test project only**.

